<ResourceDictionary
  xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
  xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
  xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"
  xmlns:po="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation/options"
  mc:Ignorable="po">

  <!--
    The glyphs of the window. Read docs/roadmap/12-minimal-ui.md, Part A, before you touch
    this file.

    Every glyph is Path geometry and never a glyph font. Step 5, fact 9, holds here. WPF
    reaches MS.Internal.Invariant.FailFast for a font family that the machine does not hold,
    and that kills the process with no dialog and no catchable exception. Wine holds no icon
    font, so an icon font would end the application on the first button that draws one.

    Every geometry starts at the origin and carries its true size. IconGlyph in Parts.xaml
    centers it and never scales it. Do not add Stretch="Uniform" to that style. WPF scales a
    stroked geometry by rules that are hard to predict at this size. See
    docs/roadmap/11-ui-polish.md, Part C.

    A frozen geometry costs one build and no copy. The software rasterizer of Wine draws
    these on the CPU, so the saving is real.
  -->

  <!--  The import button. A 12 by 12 cross.  -->
  <Geometry x:Key="IconPlus" po:Freeze="True">M 0,6 L 12,6 M 6,0 L 6,12</Geometry>

  <!--
    Move up and move down. The same chevron that ComboBoxToggleButtonStyle draws inline
    today. That template keeps its own copy, because this step adds no consumer to it.
  -->
  <Geometry x:Key="IconChevronUp" po:Freeze="True">M 0,5 L 5,0 L 10,5</Geometry>
  <Geometry x:Key="IconChevronDown" po:Freeze="True">M 0,0 L 5,5 L 10,0</Geometry>

  <!--  Remove. A lid, a handle, a body, and two ribs.  -->
  <Geometry x:Key="IconTrash" po:Freeze="True">
    M 0,3 L 13,3
    M 4.5,3 L 4.5,1 L 8.5,1 L 8.5,3
    M 1.5,3 L 2.5,13 L 10.5,13 L 11.5,3
    M 5,6 L 5,10.5
    M 8,6 L 8,10.5
  </Geometry>

  <!--
    The config button. Two rails with one knob each, which reads as a settings control.

    A gear is the other convention and it needs a dozen teeth. A tooth of that size lands
    below one pixel on the software rasterizer, and the glyph then draws as a blur.
  -->
  <Geometry x:Key="IconSettings" po:Freeze="True">
    M 0,4 L 13,4
    M 0,10 L 13,10
    M 2.6,4 A 1.6,1.6 0 1 1 5.8,4 A 1.6,1.6 0 1 1 2.6,4
    M 7.2,10 A 1.6,1.6 0 1 1 10.4,10 A 1.6,1.6 0 1 1 7.2,10
  </Geometry>

  <!--
    The warning of the action row. A triangle, a stem, and a dot.

    The dot is a small circle of two arcs and never a zero length line, for the same reason
    that IconMore below draws its dots that way.
  -->
  <Geometry x:Key="IconWarning" po:Freeze="True">
    M 7,0.8 L 13.4,12 L 0.6,12 Z
    M 7,4.8 L 7,8.2
    M 6.3,10.1 A 0.7,0.7 0 1 1 7.7,10.1 A 0.7,0.7 0 1 1 6.3,10.1
  </Geometry>

  <!--
    The spinner of the deploy button and the revert button. Three quarters of a circle,
    which is the gap that makes a rotation readable.

    <b>This geometry breaks the origin rule above on purpose.</b> A spinner turns around its
    own center, so the arc sits at the center of a 14 by 14 box. The center is 7,7 and the
    radius is 5.5. Spinner in Parts.xaml sets that box and turns the path around 0.5,0.5,
    which is the same point.
  -->
  <Geometry x:Key="IconSpinnerArc" po:Freeze="True">M 7,1.5 A 5.5,5.5 0 1 1 1.5,7</Geometry>

  <!--
    The profile menu. Three dots.

    Each dot is a small circle of two arcs and never a zero length line. A zero length
    segment draws as a dot only when the renderer applies the round cap to it, and the
    software rasterizer of Wine is not a renderer that this repository tests that rule on.
  -->
  <Geometry x:Key="IconMore" po:Freeze="True">
    M 0.4,6 A 0.9,0.9 0 1 1 2.2,6 A 0.9,0.9 0 1 1 0.4,6
    M 4.7,6 A 0.9,0.9 0 1 1 6.5,6 A 0.9,0.9 0 1 1 4.7,6
    M 9.0,6 A 0.9,0.9 0 1 1 10.8,6 A 0.9,0.9 0 1 1 9.0,6
  </Geometry>

</ResourceDictionary>
